(Stage 1b — inference soundness flip. A `value` is genuinely opaque to the
 compiler: its type travels with the payload as a runtime tag, not as
 anything this pass can prove, so appending one widens the list to Mixed.
 Reads dispatch on runtime tags; the string element proves dispatch is
 live — a fast-path int list would print "hello"'s pointer, not "hello".
 Acceptance criterion 3.

 Written until bug #45 with an undeclared-return function (`Return x add
 1.`). That is opaque too, but it carries no tag either, so the slot took a
 conservative TAG_INTEGER guess and a text-returning function read back as
 an address. That write is now refused at compile time; a `value` is the
 same opacity with the tag actually carried, so it is what this widening
 property is tested with.)

a value called tally is 5.
a list called items is [].
append "hello" to items.
append tally to items.
print element 1 of items.
print element 2 of items.
